8. FRESHNESS ESTIMATION SYSTEM
Build a visual freshness estimation system based on the produce type, harvest date, recorded storage conditions, and available tracking information.
Display freshness using clear colour-coded cards, progress bars, and labels.
Suggested freshness colours
Green — Estimated fresh The produce is within its expected shelf-life window under the assumptions used by the system.
Yellow — Freshness declining The produce is approaching its estimated shelf-life limit.
Orange — Use caution The estimated shelf-life limit is close or the available handling information suggests increased uncertainty.
Red — Estimated shelf-life limit exceeded The expected shelf-life window has passed or the produce may require additional assessment.
Grey — Insufficient data The system lacks enough reliable information to estimate freshness confidently.
Freshness calculation
Implement a configurable freshness estimation engine.
Inputs may include:
Produce type and variety.
Harvest date and time.
Current date and time.
Produce-specific expected shelf-life range.
Storage method.
Recorded temperature.
Known interruptions in refrigeration.
Transportation duration.
Handling conditions.
Missing or uncertain tracking information.
Use a configurable produce database rather than a single shelf-life value for every crop.
Examples of produce categories:
Leafy vegetables.
Root vegetables.
Tomatoes and similar fruit vegetables.
Apples and other fruits.
Bananas and other tropical fruits.
Potatoes and onions.
Grains and pulses.
Other produce.
Provide editable, documented shelf-life estimates with appropriate assumptions.
When actual storage temperatures and handling information are unavailable, show the estimate as uncertain and use conservative assumptions.
Freshness display example
"Estimated freshness: Green"
"Time since harvest: 2 days, 5 hours"
"Estimated shelf-life remaining: Approximately 3–5 days under the selected storage assumptions."
"Confidence: Limited — storage temperature history is incomplete."
All figures must be calculated from actual stored data and configured produce parameters, not hard-coded into the interface.
Important food safety limitation
Do not present estimated freshness as proof that food is safe to eat.
Freshness is an estimate, not a guarantee of food safety, quality, or remaining usable life. The system must not conclude that produce is safe solely because its status is green.
Show an appropriate disclaimer and clearly distinguish freshness estimates from actual inspections or laboratory testing.

